Undifferentiated Pleuritic Pain
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Sharp localized pain strictly worsened by deep inspiration | Clear lung fields on auscultation | Normal chest X-ray and 12-lead ECG

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-040
Reason for Visit: Acute severe distress due to sudden, sharp chest pain with breathing, requiring urgent evaluation to exclude acute pulmonary embolism or pneumothorax.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute-onset, sharp chest pain exacerbated by inspiration. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out a pulmonary embolism, acute pericarditis, or spontaneous pneumothorax, preventing life-threatening cardiopulmonary compromise prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Every time I take a breath or cough, a sharp pain stabs right into the side of my chest.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with sharp, highly localized chest discomfort. There is a distinct exacerbation of pain with deep inspiration or coughing. The patient exhibits a shallow breathing pattern to minimize the discomfort. The patient explicitly denies associated purulent sputum, fever, or severe dyspnea at rest. There is an absolute absence of recent significant thoracic trauma.
Functional Status: Resting in a chair, avoiding deep breaths.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute anxiety regarding a potential heart attack.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 124/80 mmHg | HR: 84 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 18 /min (Shallow) | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Patient appears uncomfortable when inhaling deeply but is not in respiratory distress or cyanotic. A review of recent outpatient notes reveals an unrelated history of severe nocturnal sweating, sleep disruption, and a normal infectious workup, which is currently being managed expectantly and is disconnected from tonight's acute chest pain.
Physical Exam: Respiratory examination reveals equal, bilateral air entry. There is a complete absence of a distinct pleural friction rub on auscultation. No localized wheeze or crackles. The chest wall exhibits reproducible pain on specific thoracic movements (twisting the torso). Cardiovascular exam is perfectly normal with no friction rubs, murmurs, or tachycardia. Unilateral calf swelling is absent (Homan's negative).
Environmental Observations: Clean, safe environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking no routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Undifferentiated Pleuritic Pain - Status: Acute mechanical/musculoskeletal chest wall pain.
Differentials:

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out clinically due to the complete lack of resting tachycardia, normal room-air oxygen saturation, clear lungs, and reproducible mechanical pain on torso twisting.

Spontaneous Pneumothorax - Ruled out due to equal, bilateral breath sounds, normal oxygenation, and lack of sudden extreme respiratory distress.

Acute Pericarditis - Ruled out due to normal heart sounds without a rub, lack of positional pain change (not relieved by leaning forward), and lack of typical viral prodrome.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low.

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden onset of sharp, inspiratory chest pain mandated an immediate after-hours assessment due to the inherent high risk of an acute pulmonary embolism or pneumothorax. Telephone triage correctly prioritized the necessity to evaluate acute pleurisy. A rigorous clinical examination confirmed pristine oxygenation, an absence of tachycardia, and perfectly clear lung fields. The ability to mechanically reproduce the pain via torso twisting definitively localized the pathology to the musculoskeletal chest wall rather than deep cardiopulmonary structures. By establishing this mechanical etiology and ruling out acute cardiopulmonary threats, the clinician instantly de-escalated the patient's panic and completely avoided a highly unnecessary, radiation-heavy emergency department admission for CT angiography.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed high-dose oral NSAIDs for chest wall inflammation. The expected investigation results include clear lung fields on a chest radiograph with a normal electrocardiogram, which will assist in confirming the diagnosis via an outpatient workup if symptoms persist.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce the mechanical strain on your ribs, place a firm pillow over your chest and actively squeeze it tight against yourself whenever you need to cough or sneeze; physically splinting the ribcage limits the painful expansion and speeds up the healing process.
Home-Based Services: None indicated tonight.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48 hours for a formal 12-lead ECG and chest radiograph to complete the diagnostic exclusion process.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops a sudden, uncontrollable racing heart, coughing up blood, or experiences a sudden, severe inability to catch their breath even when resting completely still.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; sharp chest pain requires formal radiographic and electrocardiographic exclusion of subtle diseases.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course involves transient, sharp pain that often resolves spontaneously or with anti-inflammatories over 5 to 7 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency attention if the pain changes from a sharp stab to a heavy, crushing sensation in the center of your chest, or if it spreads into your jaw or left arm.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: We are strictly treating your pain with anti-inflammatories tonight because your lungs and heart are working perfectly; the pain is coming entirely from the muscles and cartilage between your ribs.
Actionable Steps: Take the prescribed anti-inflammatory medication strictly with food to protect your stomach, and do not attempt any heavy lifting or twisting motions for the next 48 hours.

